 \sedar{} represents the current state-of-the-art LLM-based transfer tool in constructing initial seeds in fuzzing. 
 We used the original \sedar{} binary obtained from the authors of \sedar{}~\cite{fu2024sedar}, which runs its complete pipeline, including schema capture, end-to-end LLM translation, and mutability refinement; the latter temporarily comments out parser-incompatible fragments before mutation and restores them after mutation, before target-side execution.
 Our artifact includes the \sedar{} configuration and the \sedar{}-generated seeds used in our experiments.
+Each baseline follows its original translation workflow.
+SQLGlot and jOOQ use their built-in dialect support; CrackSQL and RISE use the official SQL documentation of each target DBMS as dialect knowledge, while \sedar{} requires no additional dialect knowledge.
+CrackSQL and RISE translate the statements of a test case individually and reassemble them in their original order without schema context, whereas \sedar{} translates complete test cases using the extracted source schemas as context.
+For a fair comparison, all methods use the same source test cases for each target DBMS (Section~\ref{sec:setup}), and no method receives additional manually written translation rules.
 
